CRPS Type I Upper Extremity
Complex Fatigue Entities
Post-injury regional sympathetic dystrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-012
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe upper limb hyperalgesia and acute vasomotor instability following a minor distal injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of excruciating burning pain, severe swelling, and inability to move the hand. Immediate assessment was required to exclude septic arthritis or acute tendon rupture, and to implement pharmacological sympathetic blockade to manage the patient's severe psychomotor distress.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hand is swollen like a balloon, freezing cold, and throbbing with a burning pain I can't tolerate.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a severe exacerbation of regional pain following a minor wrist sprain 4 weeks prior. Over the past 12 hours, the condition rapidly deteriorated. Symptoms include severe burning pain, distinct skin color changes fluctuating between dusky blue and red, and intense allodynia extending from the hand into the forearm. Patient describes a linear escalation where even passive movement of the fingers induces intense nausea and syncope-like dizziness due to pain.
Functional Status: Totally impaired right upper limb; unable to dress or prepare food.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to manipulate medication bottles or use a telephone properly with the dominant hand.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 104 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Highly anxious, cradling the right arm against the chest, fearful of any physical examination.
Physical Exam: Right hand exhibits non-pitting edema and is notably cooler to the touch compared to the left. Vasomotor instability present with livedo reticularis pattern on the dorsum of the hand. Severe hyperesthesia to light brush strokes. Range of motion in digits is severely limited by pain apprehension, though no gross structural deformity exists. Findings map to post-injury regional sympathetic dystrophy.
Environmental Observations: Medication bottles left unopened due to inability to grip.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Attempted to use ice, which drastically worsened the pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: CRPS Type I Upper Extremity - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, multi-joint diffuse involvement of the hand rather than a single joint space, and distinct temperature fluctuations (cold rather than hot).

Deep vein thrombosis of the upper limb - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain type being burning and superficial rather than deep and aching, alongside prominent sympathetic skin changes.

Peripheral neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Unilateral nature strictly bound to a preceding regional trauma, rather than a systemic glove-and-stocking distribution.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The profound upper limb pain and swelling necessitated an urgent home visit to clinically exclude rapidly progressing infections like flexor tenosynovitis or septic arthritis. Upon diagnosing sympathetically mediated pain, immediate intervention was required as the patient was in a state of severe autonomic distress (tachycardia, tachypnea) directly caused by the unmanaged pain crisis. Standard delay until regular general practice hours would have resulted in unmanageable psychological trauma and potential progression of joint contractures due to pain-induced immobility.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered single dose of oral Diazepam 5mg to reduce secondary muscle spasm and pain-related panic. Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg twice daily. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, Amitriptyline, and LDN. Warned explicitly against the use of ice packs.
Positive Behavioral Change: By gently performing passive finger stretches using your good hand for just 2 minutes every hour, you will physically prevent joint stiffening, directly reducing the buildup of inflammatory fluid and rewarding yourself with improved functional grip.
Home-Based Services Optional: Urgent occupational therapy referral for desensitization protocol.
DME/Safety Optional: Provision of pre-opened medication organizers.
Follow-up: GP review in 2 days to titrate neuropathic agents and initiate multidisciplinary pain team referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to ED if the hand develops spreading red streaks, hot and fluctuant joint swelling, or systemic fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Complex Regional Pain Syndrome requires multi-modal, long-term management; acute medications only blunt the extreme peaks of pain.
Clinical Course: The acute hyperalgesic flare will slowly stabilize over several days with the introduction of neuropathic agents. Strict avoidance of immobilization is necessary to prevent long-term disability.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately if your hand becomes fiery red, extremely hot to the touch, and you develop a fever or shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The nerves are misinterpreting normal signals as severe tissue damage; the prescribed medication stabilizes these nerves, acting as a chemical block against the burning sensation.
Actionable Steps: Keep the hand consistently warm using a loose cotton glove; protecting the limb from sudden temperature drops prevents the sympathetic nervous system from triggering painful spasms, rewarding you with a lower baseline pain level.

